MPL-2.0 in Plain Words
======================

This section is an informal summary for readers who are new to open source.
It is not legal advice and does not replace the LICENSE file.

You MAY:
- use this software for free, for both personal and commercial purposes;
- modify it, and use it in your own projects, including closed-source ones;
- redistribute it, in original or modified form.

You MUST:
- keep all license and copyright notices intact;
- when you distribute this software (in original or modified form), make the
  source code of its MPL-2.0 files, including your changes to them, available
  under MPL-2.0. Your own separate files may use any license you choose.

"Open source" does NOT mean:
- the software is in the public domain or has no owner;
- you may claim it, or any part of it, as your own work;
- the author owes you any warranty or support.

Additional Terms
================

The following terms apply in addition to MPL-2.0, to any individual, team,
company, or organization:

1. Keep notices. Do not remove or alter any license or copyright notice in
   this software (including its source code, documentation, and accompanying
   files).
2. No rebranding. Do not republish this software, or a version with only
   trivial changes (such as renaming, rebranding, or simple wrapping), under
   a different name. If you distribute a substantially modified version under
   a new name, you must clearly state that it is derived from ASON and provide
   a link to the original project.
3. No intellectual property claims. Do not use this software, in whole or in
   part, to apply for or register any patent, copyright (including software
   copyright registration), trademark, or other intellectual property right.
4. No false authorship. Do not claim this software, in whole or in part, as
   your own or independently developed work, and do not present it as your
   own research achievement (e.g., in papers, theses, project reports, or
   award applications). Using and citing it in research is welcome.
5. Copyright retained. Making the source code public does not mean giving up
   copyright. The author retains all copyrights in this software.
6. Attribution. When you use or include this software, you must credit the
   author (copyright holder) and the name "ASON" in a place where users can
   easily see it, such as the "About" page of an application or the README file.
7. Contributions. The copyright of contributions to this software belongs to
   the author (copyright holder), as described in the CONTRIBUTING file.
   Contributors do not hold any copyright in this software, in whole or in part.
8. No warranty, no support. This software is shared publicly "as is". The
   author (copyright holder) is not liable for any consequences, intended or
   not, arising from its use or inclusion, and is not obligated to provide
   technical support.

If you do not accept these terms, do not use or include this software.

Notes
=====

Some of the above terms restate what MPL-2.0 and copyright law already require;
others are additional requirements from the author. They are listed here to
explain clearly how to use this software properly, to protect the rights of
the original developer, and to ensure that the software is used in the way
the developer intended.

